window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["mlops"]=[{"id":"mlops-f0001","card":"q-mlops-001-overview","sec":"mlops-s001","q":"What is the scope of the MLOps Essentials: Model Development and Integration course?","a":"<p><strong>Build-side MLOps</strong>: requirements, data, training, model management and integration. Deployment and monitoring belong to a separate course.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0002","card":"q-mlops-001-overview","sec":"mlops-s001","q":"In MLOps Essentials: Model Development and Integration, what does the course contain instead of executable code?","a":"<p>Only flow diagrams. They are rewritten as ordered lists and tables.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0003","card":"q-mlops-002-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"In MLOps, which activities count as build activities and which as run activities?","a":"<p>Build activities: requirements, data/training pipelines, data governance, experiment tracking, integrations, model management. Run activities: infrastructure, deployment, serving, monitoring, responsible AI.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0004","card":"q-mlops-002-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"What are the build and run activities of core ML (as opposed to MLOps)?","a":"<p>Build: feature engineering, training, testing, packaging. Run: deployment and inference.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0005","card":"q-mlops-002-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"Which column of the build/run split does the MLOps Essentials: Model Development and Integration course cover?","a":"<p>The <strong>build</strong> column only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0006","card":"q-mlops-002-1-scope-big-picture","sec":"mlops-s002","q":"Why should you re-evaluate MLOps tools when you start a project?","a":"<p>The tools landscape changes fast. AWS, GCP and Azure each have their own MLOps tooling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0007","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"What kind of process is the ML life cycle, and what triggers retraining?","a":"<p>It is a <strong>cyclic</strong> process. Retraining is triggered by new training data, new user requirements, or model decay.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0008","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"What are the 8 steps of the ML life cycle, in order?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Requirements</li>\n  <li>Design</li>\n  <li>Data acquisition</li>\n  <li>Feature engineering</li>\n  <li>Model training</li>\n  <li>Testing &amp; evaluation</li>\n  <li>Deployment &amp; inference</li>\n  <li>Data collection</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0009","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"In the ML life cycle, what happens in the data collection step?","a":"<p>Production data is collected and labeled. It becomes new training data, which goes back to step 3 (data acquisition).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0010","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"In the ML life cycle, who does what in the requirements and design steps?","a":"<p>In requirements, the product owner defines the problem. In design, the workflow for executing the project is defined.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0011","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"In the ML life cycle, what happens in testing &amp; evaluation?","a":"<p>The model is refined until performance goals are met.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0012","card":"q-mlops-003-ml-life-cycle","sec":"mlops-s003","q":"What is MLOps in relation to the ML life cycle?","a":"<p>The ecosystem of processes around the cycle, not the model building itself.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0013","card":"q-mlops-004-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"How many artifact types does software engineering have, and how many does ML have?","a":"<p>Software has 2: code and records (requirements, design, test plans/results). ML has 4: code, records, <strong>data</strong> (training, production, labeled) and <strong>models</strong> (trained artifacts).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0014","card":"q-mlops-004-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"How do ML artifacts depend on each other?","a":"<p>Requirements guide data prep. Data plus requirements build the model. The model is integrated into code. Code execution produces new training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0015","card":"q-mlops-004-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"What are the challenges of managing ML artifacts compared with software?","a":"<p>Independent life cycles, coordination, collaboration, extra tooling, different skill sets, and the need for an integrated end-to-end workflow.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0016","card":"q-mlops-004-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"Why does ML need extra tooling compared with software engineering?","a":"<p>Tools and workflows are needed to manage and govern every artifact type.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0017","card":"q-mlops-004-why-ml-is-harder-than-software-engineeri","sec":"mlops-s004","q":"Why is collaboration a challenge in ML projects?","a":"<p>Data engineers, ML engineers and product owners must work together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0018","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"What is DevOps?","a":"<p>It combines development, testing and operations in one integrated workflow with agile practices and heavy <strong>automation</strong>. The same team owns all three, unlike traditional separate teams.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0019","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"What are the stages of the DevOps cycle?","a":"<p>Plan → Code → Build → Test → Release → Deploy → Operate → Monitor → Plan.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0020","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"What is MLOps, and is it a product?","a":"<p>A <strong>set of best practices</strong>, not a product or technique. It is supported by automation and tools.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0021","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"How does MLOps extend DevOps?","a":"<p>It extends DevOps to building and serving ML. It integrates data engineering and model development into the software life cycle and manages all 4 artifact types.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0022","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"How does MLOps shorten time to market?","a":"<p>It enables continuous model development and integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0023","card":"q-mlops-005-devops-vs-mlops","sec":"mlops-s005","q":"What does MLOps include beyond building models?","a":"<p>Deployment, serving, monitoring, performance analytics and feedback.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0024","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what two parallel tracks does Requirements &amp; Design split into?","a":"<p><strong>Non-ML development</strong> (APIs, services, DBs, UIs) and <strong>data engineering</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0025","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what is the role of Data Engineering and Continuous Training?","a":"<p>Data Engineering turns raw data into usable features. Continuous Training builds and refines the model until it meets requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0026","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what does Model Governance do?","a":"<p>It tracks, evaluates and manages models under a governance framework.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0027","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what is Continuous Integration?","a":"<p>Integrating the model with non-ML code at regular intervals (sprint/week).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0028","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what happens in Package &amp; Deliver, Continuous Deployment, and Serving / Monitoring?","a":"<p>Package &amp; Deliver bundles the model plus non-ML code. Continuous Deployment deploys approved packages. Serving / Monitoring serves users and keeps the model within performance thresholds.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0029","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what does the feedback loop carry and where does it go?","a":"<p>Performance, drift and bias info. It goes to model governance and requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0030","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what does data capture do?","a":"<p>Production feature/label data goes to data engineering for new datasets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0031","card":"q-mlops-006-mlops-life-cycle-software-engineering-ml","sec":"mlops-s006","q":"In the MLOps life cycle, what happens if governance decides retraining is needed?","a":"<p>It kicks off a new training cycle with new data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0032","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"What is the goal of the MLOps principles, and how should you use them?","a":"<p>The goal is an optimal end-to-end workflow that integrates teams, modules and artifacts. Use the principles as goals when adding any policy, process or tool.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0033","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"What are the 15 principles of MLOps?","a":"<p>Modular, Continuous, Automated, Incremental, End-to-end managed, Reproducible, Scalable, Observable, Traceable, Secure, Integrated, Resilient, Transparent, Collaborative, Organized.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0034","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the Modular and Continuous principles mean?","a":"<p>Modular: clear boundaries and ownership, so artifacts evolve simultaneously. Continuous: continuous development, modeling, integration and deployment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0035","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the Automated and Incremental principles mean?","a":"<p>Automated: key to efficiency and scale. Incremental: quick time to market via incremental development.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0036","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the Reproducible and Traceable principles mean?","a":"<p>Reproducible: the artifact and the code/steps to build it are both managed. Traceable: lineage of an artifact back to data and model origins.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0037","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the End-to-end managed and Observable principles mean?","a":"<p>End-to-end managed: visibility into the state of all artifacts. Observable: monitoring and diagnosis of workflows and artifacts.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0038","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the Scalable, Secure and Resilient principles mean?","a":"<p>Scalable: bigger datasets, models, teams and deployments. Secure: access controls and privacy protection. Resilient: process changes don't cascade.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0039","card":"q-mlops-007-principles-of-mlops","sec":"mlops-s007","q":"In MLOps, what do the Integrated, Transparent, Collaborative and Organized principles mean?","a":"<p>Integrated: artifacts move seamlessly across the workflow. Transparent: anyone can understand the inner workings. Collaborative: teams and artifacts collaborate. Organized: clear interfaces to outside teams, systems and processes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0040","card":"q-mlops-008-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"What are the 3 stages at which an ML effort might start MLOps?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Exploration</li>\n  <li>Experimentation</li>\n  <li>Engineering</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0041","card":"q-mlops-008-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"In the MLOps exploration stage, what is the team and what happens?","a":"<p>A team of 2–3 engineers studies the ecosystem, explores use cases, builds an initial model and pitches a strategy to executives. The project may be dropped. MLOps investment is none (ad hoc).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0042","card":"q-mlops-008-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"In the MLOps experimentation stage, what happens and how much MLOps do you invest in?","a":"<p>A bigger team picks one use case, processes data, extracts features, runs training experiments and reviews against business expectations and cost. The project may be dropped. Investment is incremental: add data/training pipelines as needed and postpone deployment/operations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0043","card":"q-mlops-008-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"In the MLOps engineering stage, who is on the team and how much MLOps do you invest in?","a":"<p>The full team (data engineers, scientists, software engineers). They set concrete requirements, design the workflow, build non-ML software, do methodical training/testing/integration, and deploy and monitor. Investment is a full MLOps system, which needs people and technology investment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0044","card":"q-mlops-008-when-to-start-mlops","sec":"mlops-s008","q":"What goes wrong if you start MLOps too early, and what if you start too late?","a":"<p>Too early wastes effort if the project is dropped. Too late brings inefficiency and integration pain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0045","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"What is the goal of an ML project, and when should you invest?","a":"<p>To improve business outcomes (more sales or lower cost). Invest only if benefits outweigh costs.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0046","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"What criteria should you use when selecting ML projects?","a":"<p>Core business value, training data availability, technology ecosystem, budget &amp; team, time to market, and risk appetite.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0047","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"When selecting an ML project, why does core business value matter?","a":"<p>The project must give strategic differentiation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0048","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"When selecting an ML project, why does training data availability matter?","a":"<p>Good data including labels is essential. Without it projects fail.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0049","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"When selecting an ML project, what does the technology ecosystem criterion require?","a":"<p>Algorithms, libraries, frameworks and pre-trained models must exist and be affordable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0050","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"When selecting an ML project, what goes wrong with budget and team, and with time to market?","a":"<p>Understaffed projects drag on and outlive their usefulness. With slow time to market, competitors may overtake you.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0051","card":"q-mlops-009-selecting-ml-projects","sec":"mlops-s009","q":"When selecting an ML project, what does risk appetite mean?","a":"<p>The organization must accept that performance or cost targets may not be met.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0052","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What do ML requirements cover and why do you write them?","a":"<p>They cover the whole ML application (ML and non-ML parts together). They keep experimentation from drifting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0053","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What are the non-ML requirement areas for an ML application?","a":"<p>User experience (GUIs and APIs), functions, deployment, scale, security and serviceability.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0054","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What do the non-ML requirements functions, scale and serviceability describe?","a":"<p>Functions: data collected, transformations, reporting, analytics. Scale: maximum capacity. Serviceability: observability and issue handling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0055","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What are the ML-specific requirements?","a":"<p>Problem statement, performance goals, operational goals and cost requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0056","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What do the ML-specific requirements problem statement, performance goals, operational goals and cost requirements mean?","a":"<p>Problem statement: e.g. \"predict customer propensity\". Performance goals: target model performance. Operational goals: how the model is accessed and used. Cost requirements: cost limits for the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0057","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"How should you state ML requirements?","a":"<p>As <strong>minimum metric values</strong> the model must achieve and maintain, in two dimensions: performance/effectiveness and operational/efficiency, for both model metrics and product metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0058","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What are example metric goals for ML requirements in each of the four cells?","a":"<p>Model performance: accuracy ≥ 90% in training. Model operational: latency ≤ 1 s (90th percentile). Product performance: conversion rate ≥ 30%. Product operational: max 500 concurrent sessions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0059","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What makes a good ML metric goal?","a":"<p>It is <strong>measurable</strong> (include instrumentation requirements), <strong>minimum required</strong>, and <strong>reasonable</strong>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0060","card":"q-mlops-010-creating-requirements","sec":"mlops-s010","q":"What should you base ML metric goals on?","a":"<p>Known performance of existing models in the domain, other market technologies, customer expectations, and what is achievable in the time available.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0061","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"What is an ML workflow?","a":"<p>A sequence of processes and iterations that develop, manage and improve ML products.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0062","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"What practices should an ML workflow design include?","a":"<p>Traceability, repeatability, automation, access controls, flexibility, ownership and autoscaling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0063","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"In an ML workflow design, what do traceability and repeatability mean?","a":"<p>Traceability: track data, models and processing steps; lineage helps measure progress and troubleshoot. Repeatability: \"data as code\" and \"model as code\" pipelines, so you can rebuild from scratch with the same results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0064","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"In an ML workflow design, what do access controls, flexibility, ownership and autoscaling mean?","a":"<p>Access controls: who can create, promote, use and discard artifacts. Flexibility: plug-and-play, replace one part without affecting the rest. Ownership: a defined owner for each pipeline part. Autoscaling: scale with data, models and teams.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0065","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"What are the decoupled sub-pipelines in an ML workflow?","a":"<p>Data engineering, model development, product development, and deployment &amp; operations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0066","card":"q-mlops-011-designing-the-ml-workflow","sec":"mlops-s011","q":"Why decouple the sub-pipelines of an ML workflow?","a":"<p>Each can evolve independently, progress in parallel, and be owned by its own sub-team. Tight coupling blocks everyone when one part has a problem.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0067","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"What is the recommended ML team composition by percentage?","a":"<p>Data engineers 30%, data scientists 20%, software engineers 20%, ML engineers 20%, operations engineers 10%.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0068","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"What does each ML team role focus on?","a":"<p>Data engineers: data processing and wrangling. Data scientists: building models. Software engineers: wrapper services and APIs around the model. ML engineers: productizing, scaling, packaging, integrations. Operations engineers: deployment and operations.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0069","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"What is the myth about ML team staffing?","a":"<p>The myth is that a couple of data scientists is enough. They are only about 20% of the team.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0070","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"How big should an ML team be, and what if the team is only 3?","a":"<p>About 10 engineers gives the right balance. With a team of 3, hire people who wear multiple hats.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0071","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"In which order should you hire an ML team?","a":"<p>Don't hire everyone upfront. Start with data engineering and data science, then add the rest once the model proves viable.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0072","card":"q-mlops-012-assembling-the-team","sec":"mlops-s012","q":"Which other people should an ML team include, and how do you handle attrition?","a":"<p>Include 1–2 domain experts and 1–2 integration leads. ML skills are in demand, so expect attrition: cross-train and document.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0073","card":"q-mlops-013-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"What is the state of the MLOps tools market?","a":"<p>Nascent, growing, and immature. Most tools are function-specific, not end-to-end, and no dominant vendor has emerged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0074","card":"q-mlops-013-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"What are examples of open source, commercial and cloud MLOps tools?","a":"<p>Open source: MLflow. Commercial: Weights &amp; Biases. Cloud: AWS, GCP, Azure.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0075","card":"q-mlops-013-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"How should you choose MLOps tools?","a":"<p>Decouple tools from the main pipeline so they can be swapped. Match tools to the use case. Try trial/free versions before buying. Check the vendor roadmap and enterprise support. Look for programmability and extensibility. Research the ecosystem at implementation time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0076","card":"q-mlops-013-choosing-tools","sec":"mlops-s013","q":"Why decouple MLOps tools from the main pipeline, and why match them to the use case?","a":"<p>Decoupling lets you swap tools. Matching matters because classical ML, NLP and vision differ.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0077","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"Why invest early in a managed data pipeline?","a":"<p>Data issues are the most common blocker for data scientists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0078","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What is the flow of a managed data pipeline?","a":"<p>Raw data (batch/streaming) → cleanse/filter/validate → transform/enrich → <strong>feature store</strong> → model training → production → new data → repeat.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0079","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What makes a data pipeline \"managed\"?","a":"<p>Engineering life cycle, environments &amp; promotion, data traceability, code versioning, observability, reproducibility, version-controlled steps, automation, and consistent state.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0080","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"In a managed data pipeline, what do engineering life cycle and environments &amp; promotion mean?","a":"<p>Engineering life cycle: treat pipeline code as production code (agile, not ad hoc). Environments &amp; promotion: separate dev/test/prod with promotion policies and deployment pipelines.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0081","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"In a managed data pipeline, what does data traceability cover?","a":"<p>Lineage: source, timeline, processing steps and exceptions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0082","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"In a managed data pipeline, what do code versioning, observability and reproducibility mean?","a":"<p>Code versioning: versioned pipeline code and deployment tracking. Observability: logging, audits, monitoring. Reproducibility: reproduce feature store contents by reprocessing raw data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0083","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"What does \"data as code\" mean in a managed data pipeline?","a":"<p>All transformations are version-controlled code, with no ad hoc manipulation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0084","card":"q-mlops-014-managed-data-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s014","q":"In a managed data pipeline, what does automation include and what is consistent state?","a":"<p>Automation: trigger on new data or schedule, auto-rollback on errors, auto-reprocess. Consistent state: the feature store stays consistent, and all errors are reported and analyzed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0085","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"Why validate new data automatically after deployment?","a":"<p>New data keeps arriving. Validate it against the original training-set patterns to catch deviations before they hurt the model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0086","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"What are the 4 types of automated data validation?","a":"<p>Feature, distribution, out-of-distribution and correlation validation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0087","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"In automated data validation, what does feature validation check?","a":"<p>Missing or erroneous data, formats and ranges. Data produced by machines can be wrong too.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0088","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"In automated data validation, what does distribution validation check?","a":"<p>Mean, std and quartiles versus the baseline, and the class distribution of categoricals, so new data belongs to the same population as training data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0089","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"In automated data validation, what does out-of-distribution validation check?","a":"<p>Outliers beyond quartiles (continuous) and new class values (categorical).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0090","card":"q-mlops-015-automated-data-validation","sec":"mlops-s015","q":"In automated data validation, what does correlation validation check?","a":"<p>Feature-target and feature-feature correlation versus baseline, which detects multicollinearity changes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0091","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"What is a feature store?","a":"<p>A <strong>centralized store of pre-processed features</strong> ready for ML, often shared by several teams and models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0092","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why use a central feature store?","a":"<p>Shared ownership with defined responsibilities. It saves cost and enables reuse.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0093","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why should a feature store have a flexible schema?","a":"<p>So you can add features without breaking consumers.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0094","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"How should related datasets be handled in a feature store?","a":"<p>Keep them separate and let consumers join at query time via linking attributes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0095","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"What is the purpose of an updated registry in a feature store?","a":"<p>It shows what exists and avoids duplicate processing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0096","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"Why should a feature store use a common format?","a":"<p>Teams customize at query time, not by changing the store. This avoids last-minute customization requests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0097","card":"q-mlops-016-managed-feature-stores","sec":"mlops-s016","q":"What storage should a feature store support?","a":"<p>Multiple storage types: files, tables, images and media, with low storage cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0098","card":"q-mlops-017-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"What is data versioning?","a":"<p>Version control for data. It creates an <strong>immutable baseline</strong> for raw, intermediate or feature datasets, at feature, record or dataset level (tool-dependent). A version can be referenced for training and testing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0099","card":"q-mlops-017-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"What are the benefits of data versioning?","a":"<p>Traceability, reproducibility, change log, rollback and parallel work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0100","card":"q-mlops-017-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"How does data versioning give traceability and reproducibility?","a":"<p>Traceability: link a data version to a specific model and experiment number. Reproducibility: recreate a model with an older data version even after the data changed.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0101","card":"q-mlops-017-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"How does data versioning give a change log and rollback?","a":"<p>Change log: shows what changed in the feature store over time. Rollback: return to the last known good state after bad processing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0102","card":"q-mlops-017-data-versioning-e-g-dvc","sec":"mlops-s017","q":"How does data versioning help parallel work?","a":"<p>Scientists use a fixed version while data evolves, so data evolution is decoupled from model development.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0103","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What is data governance?","a":"<p>It ensures <strong>integrity, security and usability</strong> of data via people, policies and processes. It applies to all data (raw, intermediate, temporary, features, metadata), at rest or in motion.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0104","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What are the elements of data governance?","a":"<p>Consistency, integrity, security, privacy, resiliency and life cycle management.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0105","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"In data governance, what do consistency and integrity mean?","a":"<p>Consistency: everyone sees a consistent snapshot once data is committed. Integrity: data is complete, accurate, conforming and error-free.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0106","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"In data governance, what do security and privacy mean?","a":"<p>Security: only authorized users can view/create/modify/delete, plus protection from adversarial attacks. Privacy: protect sensitive personal info, comply with laws/standards, and use redaction and obfuscation in the pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0107","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"In data governance, what do resiliency and life cycle management mean?","a":"<p>Resiliency: backups, standbys, redundancy, and recovery to the last known good state. Life cycle management: policies and automation from creation to cleanup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0108","card":"q-mlops-018-data-governance","sec":"mlops-s018","q":"What are the steps to implement data governance?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Identify a governance team (data owners and custodians).</li>\n  <li>Reuse existing production-data policies.</li>\n  <li>Identify all data elements in the ML pipeline.</li>\n  <li>Classify data by security and privacy needs.</li>\n  <li>Enforce through automation (scale, fewer human errors).</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0109","card":"q-mlops-019-tools-for-data-processing","sec":"mlops-s019","q":"Which tools are used for big data processing in MLOps?","a":"<p>Hadoop, Apache Spark and Apache Kafka.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0110","card":"q-mlops-019-tools-for-data-processing","sec":"mlops-s019","q":"Which databases are listed for MLOps data processing?","a":"<p>MySQL (RDBMS), and MongoDB and Cassandra (NoSQL).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0111","card":"q-mlops-019-tools-for-data-processing","sec":"mlops-s019","q":"Which tools are used for data versioning in MLOps?","a":"<p>DVC, lakeFS and Neptune.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0112","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What is the flow of a managed training pipeline?","a":"<p>Feature store → set hyperparameters → plan experiment → train → validate against performance goals → test with an independent test set → review results → if needed update parameters and retrain, until the goal is reached.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0113","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What practices make a training pipeline managed?","a":"<p>Agile life cycle, separate environments, experiment tracking, version control, reproducibility, model as code, versioned data and automation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0114","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"In a managed training pipeline, what do agile life cycle and separate environments mean?","a":"<p>Agile life cycle: a structured process adapted for continuous experiments. Separate environments: dev/test for training, with the final run in a controlled environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0115","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"In a managed training pipeline, why is experiment tracking and version control important?","a":"<p>Experiment tracking gives traceability of all experiments and is a key MLOps activity. Version control means code, data and models are versioned and tied together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0116","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What does \"model as code\" mean in a training pipeline?","a":"<p>Notebooks are baselined and versioned, so rerunning gives the same model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0117","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What does reproducibility mean in a managed training pipeline?","a":"<p>Rebuild the model from training data plus input parameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0118","card":"q-mlops-020-managed-training-pipelines","sec":"mlops-s020","q":"What can you automate in a managed training pipeline?","a":"<p>Hyperparameter tuning (grid search), auto-triggering training on new data, and comparing the new model to the baseline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0119","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is data labeling (annotation)?","a":"<p>Adding <strong>contextual tags</strong> that serve as ML targets. Example: a movie review can carry a sentiment label (\"positive\") and a rating label (4.5).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0120","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"Why does data labeling matter?","a":"<p>Unstructured data (text, images, media) often has no labels, available labels may be inaccurate or incomplete, and one dataset may need extra labels for different use cases (sentiment and recommendations).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0121","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What are the 4 methods for creating data labels?","a":"<p>Domain experts, crowdsourcing, professional annotators and programmatic labeling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0122","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"How do domain experts and professional annotators compare for labeling in scale, accuracy and cost?","a":"<p>Domain experts: low scale, high accuracy, high cost. Professional annotators: medium scale, high accuracy, high cost.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0123","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"How do crowdsourcing and programmatic labeling compare in scale, accuracy and cost?","a":"<p>Crowdsourcing: high scale, variable accuracy (knowledge/bias), low cost. Programmatic labeling: massive scale, accuracy depends on the labeling model, low cost (but time to build).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0124","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"What is the recommended approach to creating data labels?","a":"<p>Combine methods. Label a base dataset with experts or professionals, then use it to train volunteers or programs to label a much larger corpus.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0125","card":"q-mlops-021-creating-data-labels","sec":"mlops-s021","q":"Which area of MLOps is growing fast in labeling?","a":"<p>Programmatic labeling.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0126","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What is an experiment, and what does experiment tracking do?","a":"<p>Each training run is an <strong>experiment</strong>. Tracking manages the model's evolution toward the stated goals.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0127","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What should experiment tracking record for the model setup and the inputs?","a":"<p>Model setup: algorithm, architecture (deep learning), hyperparameters. Inputs: dataset and version, train/validation/test splits.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0128","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What should experiment tracking record for the outputs and the analysis?","a":"<p>Outputs: serialized model (e.g. pickle) and metrics (accuracy, errors, F1). Analysis: comparison with earlier experiments, findings and next steps.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0129","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"Why track config and inputs of experiments?","a":"<p>Any experiment can be recreated and reproduced.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0130","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"What are the benefits of experiment tracking?","a":"<p>Measure the impact of parameter or data changes, verify progress toward requirements, decide whether to promote a model, and automate promotion decisions.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0131","card":"q-mlops-022-experiment-tracking","sec":"mlops-s022","q":"Why use tools for experiment tracking instead of tracking manually?","a":"<p>Manual tracking is slow and error-prone. Use tools integrated into the training pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0132","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What is AutoML?","a":"<p>Automation of ML activities across the life cycle, enabling development, analysis and decisions without human intervention.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0133","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"How does AutoML help with feature engineering?","a":"<p>It detects and fixes issues, analyzes feature-target correlation, and selects the best features.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0134","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"How does AutoML help with model training?","a":"<p>It selects the algorithm, tunes hyperparameters, and builds ensembles (train several, pick the best).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0135","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"How does AutoML help with deployment and custom models?","a":"<p>Deployment: it judges from results whether the model is ready for production, and on drift/bias it retrains and redeploys. Custom models: it customizes a base model per customer/use case, managing hundreds of models without manual work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0136","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What are the benefits of AutoML?","a":"<p>Automated retraining without humans, faster training and deployment, better results via grid search, no need for trained data scientists to operate it, and custom models at scale.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0137","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What are the shortcomings of AutoML?","a":"<p>Errors if automation isn't fully validated, issues from new data/feature relationships, bias that may go untracked without special checks, capacity problems with multi-round grid search, and explainability that may suffer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0138","card":"q-mlops-023-automl","sec":"mlops-s023","q":"What are the best practices for AutoML?","a":"<p>Achieve <strong>manual repeatability first</strong>, pick the right pipeline tools, use experiment tracking alongside, add automated sanity tests on outputs, monitor production for degradation, and review regularly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0139","card":"q-mlops-024-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"Do MLOps principles still apply to training with generative AI, and what are the two training tasks?","a":"<p>Most MLOps principles still apply. The two tasks are pre-training and fine-tuning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0140","card":"q-mlops-024-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"What is the goal of pre-training vs fine-tuning in generative AI?","a":"<p>Pre-training creates a foundation model from scratch. Fine-tuning customizes a foundation model for a domain/task.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0141","card":"q-mlops-024-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"What data does pre-training use compared with fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Pre-training uses general-purpose data (e.g. all of Wikipedia). Fine-tuning uses enterprise, domain- or task-specific data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0142","card":"q-mlops-024-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"What does pre-training learn compared with fine-tuning?","a":"<p>Pre-training learns general patterns, linguistics and broad knowledge. Fine-tuning learns task-specific patterns that supplement or override the foundation.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0143","card":"q-mlops-024-training-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s024","q":"How do pre-training and fine-tuning compare in size, cost/time and who does them?","a":"<p>Pre-training: billions of parameters, very high cost and long time, done by few model creators (OpenAI, Google, Meta). Fine-tuning: smaller adjustments on top, much lower cost and shorter time, done by most enterprises.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0144","card":"q-mlops-025-tools-for-training","sec":"mlops-s025","q":"Which tools are used for model development in MLOps training?","a":"<p>Python and its libraries, and Jupyter Notebooks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0145","card":"q-mlops-025-tools-for-training","sec":"mlops-s025","q":"Which tool is used for source control in MLOps training?","a":"<p>Git.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0146","card":"q-mlops-025-tools-for-training","sec":"mlops-s025","q":"Which tools are used for experiment management and AutoML in MLOps training?","a":"<p>Experiment management: Kubeflow, MLflow, Weights &amp; Biases. AutoML: Kubeflow, Databricks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0147","card":"q-mlops-026-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"What is model versioning for?","a":"<p>Versions establish lineage and manage the life cycle. The scheme should resemble software versioning (numbering is org-specific).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0148","card":"q-mlops-026-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"What are linked baselines in model versioning?","a":"<p>The model version, data version and code version tied together.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0149","card":"q-mlops-026-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"What does traceability mean in model versioning?","a":"<p>From a model version, you can find its input data and hyperparameters.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0150","card":"q-mlops-026-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"Why are stable references useful in model versioning?","a":"<p>Integration and testing can use a fixed version until they finish.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0151","card":"q-mlops-026-model-versioning","sec":"mlops-s026","q":"What else does model versioning enable?","a":"<p>Performance tracking across versions, and structured life cycle management.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0152","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What is a model registry?","a":"<p>A repository for storing and tracking models: a queryable database of the model plus metadata, with status changes and audit trails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0153","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"How does a model registry compare with a feature store and a code repo?","a":"<p>It is to models what the feature store is to data and a code repo is to code. Usually one central registry is shared by all ML teams.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0154","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What does a model registry store?","a":"<p>The model artifact, metadata, hyperparameters, status, audit trail and lineage links.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0155","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What metadata and artifact does a model registry store?","a":"<p>Model artifact: serialized model (e.g. pickle). Metadata: unique ID, version, description. Hyperparameters: those used for this model.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0156","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What do status, audit trail and lineage links mean in a model registry?","a":"<p>Status: current life cycle stage. Audit trail: timestamps of state changes, test pass/fail and deployment. Lineage links: input data version, code/notebook version and experiment-tracking results.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0157","card":"q-mlops-027-model-registry","sec":"mlops-s027","q":"What are the MLOps benefits of a model registry?","a":"<p>Traceability (recreate the model), life cycle tracking, collaboration across teams and time, automation of model creation and consumption, and governance (access control, auditing).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0158","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What is model benchmarking, and when does it happen?","a":"<p>It compares a model and its versions against <strong>baselines and competing models</strong>, after the model passes its fitness test and before integration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0159","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"Why benchmark in an environment closer to production?","a":"<p>It measures both performance and operational metrics. A model that looks good in a notebook may need more resources or add latency there.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0160","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What are the components of a benchmarking setup?","a":"<p>Hardware, isolated environment, test harness, test executors, tracking platform and mode.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0161","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"In a model benchmarking setup, what do hardware and isolated environment mean?","a":"<p>Hardware: production-like CPU, memory and disk. Isolated environment: software and network isolated from other activity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0162","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"In a model benchmarking setup, what do test harness and test executors do?","a":"<p>Test harness: the same test dataset and expected results for the baseline and new model. Test executors: run cases with required pacing and capacity.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0163","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"In a model benchmarking setup, what do the tracking platform and mode mean?","a":"<p>Tracking platform: logs predictions, latency, errors and resource use, and computes metrics. Mode: both models simultaneously, or one model against a stored baseline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0164","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"Which metrics does benchmarking measure?","a":"<p><strong>Performance</strong> (accuracy, F1, precision, recall) and <strong>operational</strong> (latency, scaling, resource utilization).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0165","card":"q-mlops-028-benchmarking-models","sec":"mlops-s028","q":"What are the best practices for benchmarking models?","a":"<p>Apples-to-apples conditions, log everything at the finest granularity, automate and integrate into pipelines, and use experiment tracking tools for long-term analytics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0166","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"What is the sample model life cycle flow?","a":"<p>Training → Created → Benchmarking (pass: Passed, fail: Discarded) → Integration testing (pass: Approved, fail: Archived) → Deployment (Deployed) → replaced by a new version (Retired). Customize it per organization.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0167","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"In the model life cycle, how does a model reach the Created, Passed and Discarded states?","a":"<p>Created: training produces it. Passed: it passes benchmarking. Discarded: it fails benchmarking.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0168","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"In the model life cycle, how does a model reach the Approved, Archived, Deployed and Retired states?","a":"<p>Approved: it passes integration testing. Archived: it fails integration testing. Deployed: an approved model goes to production. Retired: a new version replaces it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0169","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"When should you define the model life cycle and promotion criteria?","a":"<p>During requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0170","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"What should model promotions be based on?","a":"<p>The performance and operational metrics from requirements.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0171","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"What are the integration-test pass criteria for a model?","a":"<p>They include ML metrics <strong>and</strong> non-ML criteria (e.g. total issues found).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0172","card":"q-mlops-029-model-life-cycle-management","sec":"mlops-s029","q":"How should you handle model promotion automation and records?","a":"<p>Automate promotions on metric thresholds. Add human oversight at first and reduce it as the pipeline matures. Record decisions and promotion history in the registry.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0173","card":"q-mlops-030-tools-for-model-management","sec":"mlops-s030","q":"Which tools are used for model registry in MLOps?","a":"<p>MLflow, Neptune and Weights &amp; Biases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0174","card":"q-mlops-030-tools-for-model-management","sec":"mlops-s030","q":"Which tools are used for model benchmarking and AutoML in MLOps?","a":"<p>Benchmarking: MLPerf, DAWNBench, MLflow. AutoML: Kubeflow, Databricks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0175","card":"q-mlops-030-tools-for-model-management","sec":"mlops-s030","q":"Why keep the number of model management tools low?","a":"<p>Each tool needs administration, integration and maintenance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0176","card":"q-mlops-031-solution-integration-pipeline","sec":"mlops-s031","q":"Why does a model need integration with other code?","a":"<p>It must be embedded in other code (APIs, UIs, databases, microservices) to deliver a solution.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0177","card":"q-mlops-031-solution-integration-pipeline","sec":"mlops-s031","q":"What are the steps of a solution integration pipeline?","a":"<ol>\n  <li>Feature store plus hyperparameters → train model → <strong>model registry</strong>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Notebook to software</strong> conversion → regression testing → ML service ready for integration.</li>\n  <li>Non-ML code (APIs, UIs, DBs) evolves independently in parallel.</li>\n  <li>Integration testing → performance testing → <strong>package &amp; deliver</strong> (model executable plus non-ML code).</li>\n</ol>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0178","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What is the goal of converting a notebook to software?","a":"<p>To convert notebook code into a deployable, executable model that faithfully reproduces notebook processing and performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0179","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What are the embedded deployment forms of a model?","a":"<p>Embedded in non-ML executables: a function in a program, an SDK, a Python package, a pipeline Lambda function, or bundled in a product.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0180","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What are the service deployment forms of a model?","a":"<p>Batch: a batch ML job (reads from DB, writes predictions back). Batch/streaming: a REST API (request in, prediction out). Streaming: a stream processing job (queue in, queue out).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0181","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What must you convert when turning a notebook into software?","a":"<p>Pre-processing, model file, inference, post-processing, interfaces, and build/test/deploy infra.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0182","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What do pre-processing and post-processing include in notebook-to-software conversion?","a":"<p>Pre-processing: tokenization, encoding, input scaling. Post-processing: decoding, reverse scaling, conversion to business outcomes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0183","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"Where does the model file come from in notebook-to-software conversion?","a":"<p>From the registry. It is packaged or read from an external repo at runtime.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0184","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What are interfaces and build/test/deploy infra in notebook-to-software conversion?","a":"<p>Interfaces: function calls, REST APIs or DB queries for input/output. Build/test/deploy infra: CI/CD like any other software module.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0185","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"How do you run a regression test after converting a notebook to software?","a":"<p>Use the model's benchmark results as the baseline and the same benchmark input data, and verify the same performance as the notebook. If not, fix and retest.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0186","card":"q-mlops-032-notebook-to-software","sec":"mlops-s032","q":"What should you pay special attention to in the regression test, and what tools can help?","a":"<p>Pre- and post-processing. Some tools convert notebooks to services automatically, so try those first.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0187","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"Why do you need integration patterns for ML and non-ML parts?","a":"<p>ML and non-ML parts evolve simultaneously. Example: ML side Model v2.0 → v3.0 while non-ML side goes v4.0 → v5.0.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0188","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"What is Option 1 for integrating ML and non-ML code?","a":"<p>Hold ML constant: use ML Service v2.0 through all of non-ML v5.0 development and release, and introduce v3.0 in the next release.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0189","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"What is Option 2 for integrating ML and non-ML code?","a":"<p>Adapt the new ML ASAP: use v2.0 until v3.0 exists, then swap mid-cycle.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0190","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"How do the integration options compare on stability and time to market?","a":"<p>Option 1 (hold ML constant): stable development, but slower time to market (two separate releases). Option 2 (adapt new ML ASAP): a new version mid-cycle may cause problems, but time to market is faster overall.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0191","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"How do the integration options compare on issue detection, collaboration and automation?","a":"<p>Option 1: issues found later, simpler collaboration, straightforward automation. Option 2: issues found earlier, ML and non-ML teams must coordinate closely, and automation is hard because auto-pulling new versions may break the pipeline.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0192","card":"q-mlops-033-integration-patterns","sec":"mlops-s033","q":"How do you choose between the integration options?","a":"<p>Choose by requirements and mitigate the weaknesses with process.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0193","card":"q-mlops-034-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"What are the best practices for integrating ML and non-ML code?","a":"<p>Define code promotion policies for each stage, define measurable acceptance criteria (pass/fail tests or metrics), automate integration and testing, run both ML and non-ML test cases, and keep ML and non-ML teams informed of each other's work.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0194","card":"q-mlops-034-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"Why automate integration and testing of ML and non-ML code?","a":"<p>Both sides change frequently.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0195","card":"q-mlops-034-best-practices-for-integration","sec":"mlops-s034","q":"What should acceptance criteria be for ML integration?","a":"<p>Measurable, as pass/fail tests or metrics.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0196","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"How are GenAI models usually consumed, and what does integration work mean?","a":"<p>GenAI models are huge, so most are consumed through a cloud service (OpenAI) or hosted on AWS/GCP, even when open source. Integration work means testing the GenAI app with the model service.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0197","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"What are the areas to test in GenAI integration?","a":"<p>Model integration, prompts, vector databases, AI agent tools, guardrails and performance.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0198","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"In GenAI integration, why are prompts important to test?","a":"<p>They are the integration code between the app and the model. Test them in the application and cover them fully if users can change them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0199","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"In GenAI integration, what do you test for model integration and vector databases?","a":"<p>Model integration: resource and bandwidth provisioning, and security. Vector databases: part of RAG apps, so include them in integration tests.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0200","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"In GenAI integration, what do you test for AI agent tools and guardrails?","a":"<p>AI agent tools: the tools and their integration points with external apps. Guardrails: privacy protection, hallucination and harmful-content filtering work in production.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0201","card":"q-mlops-035-integration-with-generative-ai","sec":"mlops-s035","q":"In GenAI integration, why test performance?","a":"<p>Latency and cost affect user experience and budget.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0202","card":"q-mlops-036-8-tools-cheat-sheet-principles","sec":"mlops-s036","q":"Which tools are listed for data processing, databases and data versioning in the MLOps cheat sheet?","a":"<p>Data processing: Hadoop, Apache Spark, Apache Kafka. Databases: MySQL, MongoDB, Cassandra. Data versioning: DVC, lakeFS, Neptune.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0203","card":"q-mlops-036-8-tools-cheat-sheet-principles","sec":"mlops-s036","q":"Which tools are listed for model development, experiment management and model registry in the MLOps cheat sheet?","a":"<p>Model development: Python, Jupyter Notebooks, Git. Experiment management: MLflow, Kubeflow, Weights &amp; Biases. Model registry: MLflow, Neptune, Weights &amp; Biases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0204","card":"q-mlops-036-8-tools-cheat-sheet-principles","sec":"mlops-s036","q":"Which tools are listed for benchmarking and AutoML in the MLOps cheat sheet?","a":"<p>Benchmarking: MLPerf, DAWNBench, MLflow. AutoML: Kubeflow, Databricks.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"mlops-f0205","card":"q-mlops-036-8-tools-cheat-sheet-principles","sec":"mlops-s036","q":"What is the next step the course recommends after MLOps Essentials: Model Development and Integration?","a":"<p>Take \"MLOps Essentials: Model Deployment and Monitoring\" for the run side, and try free trials of the tools.</p>","hand":true}];
